Synced DHCP domains between hand entries, across lanes.

The corp lane's synced DHCP domains rank by depth like any hand entry: every
suffix of the three lanes goes to the rule-set of its label count, and the
router consults the deeper bands first (rowt_core::render::RULE_SETS_DIR has
the argument). So a synced 3-label domain under escape's broader 2-label
`shadow.example` wins by depth, as it did inline before contributor PR #5 —
which had put the synced domains in one set after every inline suffix, so that
`x.hq.shadow.example` briefly routed escape. `explain` must rank the same way,
and both implementations must agree on it:

    x.hq.shadow.example      ->  CORP    (synced hq.shadow.example, 3 labels, over escape's shadow.example, 2)
    x.synced.corp.example    ->  CORP    (synced.corp.example over the head's corp.example: same lane, the deeper entry named)
    a.dot.synced.example     ->  CORP    (the dot-led synced entry; nothing else covers it)

The CIDR block is there so the corp-cidr set carries a synced CIDR next to the
hand-added one.
